import eliot.collection.List
import eliot.build.model.PackageId
import eliot.build.model.Version

/**
 * The typed `eliot.pkg` model: what a descriptor *says*, as data.
 *
 * Nothing here knows the file's syntax. `eliot.build.format.Clause` decides what shape a descriptor
 * has and `eliot.build.format.PackageFile` decides what the shapes mean; this module is only the
 * vocabulary the two of them agree on, and the one the resolver reads. That is why it sits in
 * `model` with the rest of the domain: a consumer that holds a descriptor — resolution above all —
 * imports these types and drags no parser behind it.
 *
 * **A descriptor is a list of packages and nothing else.** There are no scopes: the base, test and
 * artifact dependency lists this model used to carry were four ways of writing one thing, held apart
 * by three rules about what may not travel to a consumer. A package's dependencies reach whatever
 * depends on *that package*, and nothing depends on a suite or an executable, so the isolation is the
 * shape of the graph rather than something the model has to state (`docs/build-system.md`, "What this
 * replaced, and why").
 */

/**
 * A dependency: either a *sibling* — a package of the repository being read, which versions with that
 * repository and so carries no version of its own — or a *requirement* on a foreign package at a
 * *minimum* version. Never a range and never an upper bound — resolution picks the maximum of the
 * declared minimums, so a version token is only ever a floor. Two constructors rather than an
 * optional version, because the version is absent exactly when the target is a sibling, and a shape
 * that could say otherwise would leave the resolver a case to refuse.
 */
data Dependency =
   SiblingDependency(dependencyPackage: PackageName) |
   Requirement(requiredPackage: Package, minimumVersion: Version)

/**
 * A package: a directory of sources, what it compiles against, what a build of it runs, and the
 * compiler plugins it ships.
 *
 * `exported` decides whether anything outside the repository may depend on it; examples, applications
 * and test fixtures are `internal`. It is not about what travels — a package's dependencies travel to
 * whoever deps *it*, and nothing deps a suite — but about who is allowed to name it at all.
 *
 * `packageRoot` is the package's **root directory**, relative to the repository root, and the two
 * source roots hang under it: sources at `<packageRoot>/src`, the compile-time overlay at
 * `<packageRoot>/compiler`. It is always populated — the package's own name where the descriptor says
 * nothing, which is why the root package writes `at .` — so a consumer reads a directory rather than
 * folding an absence and re-deriving the default at every use. The default is a *reading* of the `at` clause and
 * belongs to whoever reads one; by the time a descriptor is a value, where its packages live is
 * settled.
 *
 * It is text rather than a `Path` because it is text in the *repository's* syntax, read out of a git
 * object database and resolved against a checkout root that this model has never seen. Whoever
 * assembles sources owns that resolution, and owns the platform's path type with it.
 *
 * `packageCompiler` is the package's one `compiler` line: the words a build passes to the compiler,
 * verbatim, for every package whose closure holds this one (`docs/build-system.md`, "What a build
 * runs"). A list of words and nothing the launcher interprets — no placeholder, no template — which is
 * what keeps it inert data. Absent on a package that runs nothing, which is most of them.
 *
 * `packageAssets` are the names of the *release assets* of this package — compiler plugins — and the
 * name is the whole location. A release asset hangs off the same repository at the same tag the
 * sources came from, so a consumer that has already chosen a version has already chosen the binary,
 * and sources and plugin cannot skew. Nothing says what an asset *is*: every asset in a closure goes
 * on the compiler's classpath, and the compiler knows which of its plugins is which. No hash,
 * deliberately: an asset is built from the tagged tree *after* the tag exists, so no hash of it can
 * live in the commit that tag names. `eliot.lock` records one on first fetch.
 */
data BuildPackage(
   packageName: PackageName,
   packageRoot: String,
   exported: Bool,
   packageDependencies: List[Dependency],
   packageCompiler: Option[List[String]],
   packageAssets: List[String]
)

/**
 * Everything an `eliot.pkg` says: the packages inside one repository.
 *
 * Deliberately absent: the repository's own name (the URL is the identity), its version (the git tag
 * carries it), and a toolchain directive (the toolchain is an ordinary dependency).
 *
 * **A package exists exactly when the file declares it**, the root package among them: `root` is the
 * one a `dep` line carrying no selector selects, and it is written out like every other package,
 * `package root { at . … }`. A repository whose packages all live in subdirectories declares no root
 * package, and a bare `dep` on it names a package it does not declare.
 */
data Descriptor(packages: List[BuildPackage])

/**
 * The package `name` declares, absent where the descriptor declares none — the one lookup every
 * reader of a descriptor makes, so it is written once here rather than in each of them.
 */
def packageNamed(name: PackageName, descriptor: Descriptor): Option[BuildPackage] =
   descriptor.packages.find(built -> built.packageName == name)
